  "summary": "Elaborately carved wooden doors characteristic of Bukhara and surrounding regions, featuring intricate geometric and floral patterns in high relief.",
  "description": "These doors were typically crafted from mulberry or walnut wood, with artisans creating complex interlocking patterns and calligraphic elements using specialized chisels and techniques. The tradition flourished particularly during the Shaybanid period, with doors serving as important status symbols in both religious and secular buildings.",
  "culturalContext": "Bukhara's carved wooden doors — the Central Asian craft at its zenith (14th-19th c.) — made every mosque, madrasah and merchant house a threshold statement: deep-relief geometric star panels, linked kufic cartouches, dua-hand handles, and pine-and-elm carving so dense it reads as textile. Masters (khoja nurlon) signed lintels; the Ark citadel's doors and Bolo Hauz portals are canons, and Bukhara's workshop quarter (UNESCO intangible listing) still carves for trade and export.",
